WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.082 --> 00:00:01.280
[Upbeat music begins]

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:07.120
Our research focuses on AI and&nbsp;
machine learning for processing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.983
both biomedical text and image&nbsp;data.

00:00:09.983 --> 00:00:15.112
Using machine learning and AI techniques, we address a set of different problems.

00:00:15.112 --> 00:00:16.798
So&nbsp;these are different examples.

00:00:16.798 --> 00:00:21.920
We find that it's increasingly difficult for individual&nbsp;
researchers to keep up with the rapid growth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.520
of the biomedical literature. We're talking about&nbsp;
over a million articles in PubMed every year and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.746
that means two or three papers every minute.

00:00:29.746 --> 00:00:33.773
We&nbsp;developed a new search algorithm called "Best&nbsp;match".

00:00:33.773 --> 00:00:38.880
Our priority is to put the most relevant&nbsp;
papers at the top of their search results in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.520
first page or in the second page, because&nbsp;
that's where most of the user action is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.938
and this algorithm is used in PubMed by millions&nbsp;
of users on a daily basis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.938 --> 00:00:54.720
The second example is our response to the COVID-19 pandemic. At the&nbsp;
beginning of the pandemic, it was &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:02.480
difficult to locate all the papers on COVID-19 because the term&nbsp;
"COVID-19" was not used by the research community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:07.920
until later in that year. As a result, there are&nbsp;
many different expressions used in the literature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:14.160
to refer to the COVID-19 pandemic. So a simple&nbsp;
keyword search would miss many relevant&nbsp;papers.&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.120
In response, we put together a database&nbsp;
called "LitCovid" using machine learning and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:24.898
in particular, text classification algorithms, that&nbsp;
go beyond keyword matching, that would find &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.898 --> 00:01:31.920
all the papers relevant to COVID-19 regardless what&nbsp;
kind of expressions were used by the authors. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:37.835
My last example is our joint work with our clinical&nbsp;
researchers at the National Eye Institute.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.835 --> 00:01:45.302
We developed an AI tool called "DeepSeeNet" formation&nbsp;
diagnosis and prognosis in retinal diseases such&nbsp;as&nbsp;

00:01:45.302 --> 00:01:52.160
age-related macular degeneration. Not only can&nbsp;
we use retinal images for predicting eye diseases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:58.320
we can use retinal images to gain insights&nbsp;
for other systematic diseases in other parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:03.440
of the body. In one of the recent works, we used&nbsp;
retinal images to predict heart attack with very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:09.520
high accuracy and right now we are pursuing a&nbsp;
dissimilar project where we use retinal images&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:15.646
to predict brain diseases such as dementia and&nbsp;
cognitive function decline. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.646 --> 00:02:22.560
In the long run, what we really aim to do is to teach computers to read&nbsp;
and understand scientific papers like scientists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:29.200
to interpret x-rays or retinal images&nbsp;
like radiologists and ophthalmologists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:35.840
for disease diagnosis at a speed and at a&nbsp;
accuracy that's above and beyond human ability.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:38.718
[Upbeat Music]

00:02:38.718 --> 00:02:42.440
[Music fades to silence]

